2.	AI Leads RoundTable (CIB part)

Q1. CIB’s transformation journey with AI
•	AI becoming BAU in CIB → Today, AI must become part of day-to-day business operations, embedded in processes
•	Split between AI Lab and IT :
	The AI Lab now focuses on innovation, data science, and exploring new technologies (ex Agentic AI).
	The IT teams are responsible for integrating AI into core business processes and being autonomous in running and maintaining AI.
•	GenAI adoption: LLMs deployed to all CIB staff → encourage testing and integration AI tools into processes and daily work.
•	AI Lab role: 
•	selecting the right models, evaluating approaches, and testing emerging technologies (eg Agentic AI).
•	Act as a coordinator between IT and business lines to ensure innovation can be implemented.
•	Transformation :
•	AI is not only about replacing manual tasks with automation, it often requires rethinking and redesigning entire processes.
•	the AI Lab is expanding its scope to include upstream and downstream process analysis, not just modeling.

Q2. CIB’s governance practices for AI

1.	 Multi-layer governance
•	Governance involves not only technical teams but also control functions (risk, compliance, etc.).
•	A dedicated CIB AI control team was created to oversee AI strategy and governance.
2.  Business involvement
Business units are no longer just “users” of AI, they must act as AI model owners ->  responsible for monitoring performance, tracking value creation, and ensuring long-term relevance of models.
3. Skill-up across org
•	It’s not enough for IT and Lab teams to understand AI, Control functions and business stakeholders must also develop AI knowledge (risks and governance requirements).

Q3. What needs to be in place before deploying Agentic AI at CIB
1.	Process understanding & redesign
•	Before applying Agentic AI, teams must deeply analyze and understand the process. It’s not enough to simply add Agentic AI to an existing workflow.
•	Sometimes, introducing Agentic AI means the process itself needs to be re-engineered or redesigned.
	Example: instead of automating the old process, it may need to build a new process where Agentic AI plays a core role.
2.	IT, data, and security requirements
•	Agentic AI relies on tools, workflows, databases, and high-quality data.
•	Security is critical:
	Example: in one of the use cases, AgenticAI automatically answer client requests. Before providing any information, the system must ensure that the requester is entitled to receive the answer (it must verify the requester’s identity and entitlements) -> requires a strong security layer.
	This means the referentials (who can see what, who is authorized) must be accurate and up-to-date. Ensuring these access rights are properly defined and enforced is a prerequisite for deploying Agentic AI
3.	Monitoring and control of Agentic AI behavior
•	Once Agentic AI is deployed, there must be continuous monitoring to detect and prevent unintended or risky behaviors.




3	. Articulatory Agentic AI in CIB

1.	Agentic AI : From Hype to Reality
•	Today, Agentic AI is at the top of the hype cycle, widely discussed in banking and beyond. The challenge: current approaches are not delivering the expected value.
•	There is a massive gap between what we can build and what the business actually needs.
•	A better way: Start with technology-centric prototypes -> do many fast iterations towards business goals -> build small but useful components instead of massive projects -> engage early with users to manage expectations -> Later, combine components into bigger assistants with better UX.
2.	Agentic AI in CIB : Current Initiatives
•	Major transformation projects already integrating Agentic AI :
•	Global Markets: use Agentic to generate insights and cross-analyze market data.
•	Global Banking: smarter document processing → extracting, mapping, and interacting with information.
•	Client Support: automate query handling and autonomously answer client requests.
•	Key use cases : Sales Assistant (helps sales navigate huge, complex datasets), Web Agent (extract in an autonomous way from company websites and generate structured summaries), Smart Query Manager (automates large part of client queries -> verify client identity and entitlements).
3.	Risks and Governance of AgenticAI
•	LLM risks + new risks due to autonomy : resource misuse, uncontrolled or excessive app access, conflicting actions between multiple agents, compounding errors → amplified hallucinations, reward hacking → unintended behaviors, over-delegation → human in the loop.
4. AI Trends in Financial Sector (Artefact)
1. Responsible AI: The necessary pillar before going any further
Responsible AI in the financial sector is the foundation -> 6 pillars :
•	Human Agency, Accountability & Oversight : AI should remain an enabler of human judgment, not a substitute. Define protocols ensuring that humans are “in the loop” to supervise, validate, and make final decisions.
•	Privacy & Data Governance : AI must rely on compliant, high-quality data.
•	Technical Robustness, Accuracy & Safety : AgenticAI is powerful but can sometimes produce errors -> implement observability platforms to continuously monitor AI systems
•	Transparency and Copyright 
•	Diversity, Fairness & Non-Discrimination
•	Societal, Environmental & Cost Oversight
2.Traditional AI vs Agentic AI
•	Traditional AI (today’s AI) : mainly answers questions in real time (eg : LLMs, Chatbots)
•	Agentic AI : goes beyond answering, it can take actions (e.g., send emails, classify document).
•	Agent Process Automation (APA) : manage processes instead of simply executing predefined tasks.

3.Types of Agents
•	Task Agents: perform a single, well-defined action (e.g., send an email, classify a document).
•	Workflow Agents: Coordinate multiple Task Agents to complete a multi-step process (e.g., fraud case resolution, credit approval, account opening).



